WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:02.010
- You were forced to
make creative decisions

00:00:02.010 --> 00:00:04.320
based on the rules that you're given,

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:05.910
which is like you can't go back

00:00:05.910 --> 00:00:08.190
and just do the same tricks over and over,

00:00:08.190 --> 00:00:10.052
even the same looks.

00:00:10.052 --> 00:00:12.969
(foreboding music)

00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:23.550
(foreboding music continues)

00:00:29.730 --> 00:00:31.980
First and foremost,
those two movies gave me

00:00:31.980 --> 00:00:35.190
my initial relationship with James Wan.

00:00:35.190 --> 00:00:39.480
To watch James and Leigh
reinvigorate the genre,

00:00:39.480 --> 00:00:42.660
move the genre forward
and challenge themselves

00:00:42.660 --> 00:00:44.400
from the guys who created "Saw"

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:47.130
to come back and really
turn this world on its head.

00:00:47.130 --> 00:00:49.110
There was something about
the first "Insidious" movie

00:00:49.110 --> 00:00:51.420
where because we're on
such a shoestring budget

00:00:51.420 --> 00:00:53.070
shot over 20 something days,

00:00:53.070 --> 00:00:55.050
you were forced to make creative decisions

00:00:55.050 --> 00:00:57.510
based on the rules that you're given,

00:00:57.510 --> 00:00:59.250
which is like turn off the lights,

00:00:59.250 --> 00:01:00.960
that's the further in camera,

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:03.690
practical scares on a modest budget,

00:01:03.690 --> 00:01:05.569
all those things that I really learned

00:01:05.569 --> 00:01:07.140
from acting in the movies.

00:01:07.140 --> 00:01:09.210
So it was a lot more
about just the concept

00:01:09.210 --> 00:01:10.740
of how these "Insidious" movies work

00:01:10.740 --> 00:01:14.636
that I think really reflect
in how I approached this one.

00:01:14.636 --> 00:01:17.636
(suspenseful music)

00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:22.080
You can't go back and
just do the same tricks

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:23.640
over and over even the same looks.

00:01:23.640 --> 00:01:25.890
Even the budget of the
first "Conjuring" film

00:01:25.890 --> 00:01:27.210
it was a whole different ballgame

00:01:27.210 --> 00:01:29.640
from what we had on "Insidious" movies

00:01:29.640 --> 00:01:32.820
and there's an expanse to
"The Conjuring" franchise

00:01:32.820 --> 00:01:34.860
just simply because of money and time.

00:01:34.860 --> 00:01:37.980
Even Leigh Whannell said to
me about this movie is that,

00:01:37.980 --> 00:01:40.770
it felt bigger than
other "Insidious" movies,

00:01:40.770 --> 00:01:44.640
and I think a lot of that
is my scope of a film

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:47.310
is not to keep it tiny.

00:01:47.310 --> 00:01:49.650
I wanna be efficient and a
lot of this does take place

00:01:49.650 --> 00:01:51.360
in his dorm room and those actions,

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:53.340
but I knew two people
with different coasts,

00:01:53.340 --> 00:01:56.343
I wanted different looks, I
wanted daytime and nighttime.

00:01:57.240 --> 00:01:59.396
I wanted different feels.

00:01:59.396 --> 00:02:01.980
This movie is a little more expansive

00:02:01.980 --> 00:02:03.870
and I think that's what
"Conjuring" taught me

00:02:03.870 --> 00:02:06.330
that while sometimes it's
great to get down and dirty

00:02:06.330 --> 00:02:08.250
and do a movie in a couple weeks,

00:02:08.250 --> 00:02:09.870
it's also nice to have time,

00:02:09.870 --> 00:02:12.642
certainly to be able to build out scares

00:02:12.642 --> 00:02:14.580
which really have to be your set pieces

00:02:14.580 --> 00:02:15.813
that define your movie.

00:02:17.107 --> 00:02:20.107
(suspenseful music)

00:02:21.150 --> 00:02:22.860
I just emailed with David the other day,

00:02:22.860 --> 00:02:25.590
I mean, that's still one
of my proudest movies.

00:02:25.590 --> 00:02:27.900
I think the efficiency, that doesn't sound

00:02:27.900 --> 00:02:30.840
like a very creative word,
but after you've directed,

00:02:30.840 --> 00:02:32.850
it's an incredibly valuable word.

00:02:32.850 --> 00:02:35.250
We shot "Hard Candy" in 18 1/2 days.

00:02:35.250 --> 00:02:36.780
I didn't even know you could do that.

00:02:36.780 --> 00:02:39.150
I had come off of two
"Phantom of the Opera"

00:02:39.150 --> 00:02:42.570
and the "Alamo," both these
just massive movies to me

00:02:42.570 --> 00:02:44.310
that were six months at a time.

00:02:44.310 --> 00:02:46.020
And all of a sudden, you're
gonna shoot this whole thing

00:02:46.020 --> 00:02:47.433
in 18 1/2 days.

00:02:48.390 --> 00:02:49.470
And it was David's first film.

00:02:49.470 --> 00:02:52.290
What the movie lacked in expanse

00:02:52.290 --> 00:02:54.870
and maybe even coverage and time,

00:02:54.870 --> 00:02:57.990
he made up on the backend
with his color palette.

00:02:57.990 --> 00:03:00.270
I think one scene maybe was cut.

00:03:00.270 --> 00:03:03.180
I mean, he was so efficient and lien

00:03:03.180 --> 00:03:06.150
in what he needed to make his story.

00:03:06.150 --> 00:03:09.270
His efficiency of
shooting and color palette

00:03:09.270 --> 00:03:12.279
was something that I
certainly admire to this day.

00:03:12.279 --> 00:03:15.279
(suspenseful music)

00:03:17.400 --> 00:03:20.460
One of the things that I
always loved about Stephen King

00:03:20.460 --> 00:03:23.250
that I think has a real
throughline with "Insidious"

00:03:23.250 --> 00:03:27.630
is having a very dark undercurrent

00:03:27.630 --> 00:03:31.350
of some kind of emotional
or family distress,

00:03:31.350 --> 00:03:35.010
and then completely
going balls to the wall,

00:03:35.010 --> 00:03:40.010
wacky, supernatural, strange
from outer space aliens

00:03:40.440 --> 00:03:41.520
in the same film.

00:03:41.520 --> 00:03:42.660
I really love that.

00:03:42.660 --> 00:03:44.820
I think this genre supports that

00:03:44.820 --> 00:03:47.340
and no one writes that
better than Stephen King.

00:03:47.340 --> 00:03:52.200
What "Insidious" is is having
this very core of this family

00:03:52.200 --> 00:03:55.350
and in my movies, this family
dealing with the trauma

00:03:55.350 --> 00:03:56.340
of nine years ago.

00:03:56.340 --> 00:03:57.870
That's why I didn't mind taking

00:03:57.870 --> 00:04:02.190
these very melodramatic
turns in the final act

00:04:02.190 --> 00:04:04.800
because the "Insidious"
world warrants that.

00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:06.840
I think it's okay in "Insidious,"

00:04:06.840 --> 00:04:08.280
wouldn't have done it in "The Conjuring,"

00:04:08.280 --> 00:04:09.390
different kind of thing.

00:04:09.390 --> 00:04:12.000
Often actors and storytellers,
certainly from the theater,

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:14.640
they run from words
like camp or melodrama.

00:04:14.640 --> 00:04:16.590
I think the supernatural element

00:04:16.590 --> 00:04:18.840
of it is important to
the "Insidious" world.

00:04:18.840 --> 00:04:21.960
James really dug into like
carnival souls and some,

00:04:21.960 --> 00:04:24.120
even his makeup design and that.

00:04:24.120 --> 00:04:25.920
And while I didn't really do that,

00:04:25.920 --> 00:04:28.200
I certainly feel an
influence of Stephen King,

00:04:28.200 --> 00:04:29.700
the painting at the end.

00:04:29.700 --> 00:04:31.920
To me, I just felt like, sure, yeah,

00:04:31.920 --> 00:04:34.170
let's do something
really bold and strange.

00:04:34.170 --> 00:04:37.380
And also I had to confine
it into a space for time

00:04:37.380 --> 00:04:38.220
and everything else.

00:04:38.220 --> 00:04:40.560
So I was actually trying to think

00:04:40.560 --> 00:04:43.920
of the Stephen King element
that make really like get

00:04:43.920 --> 00:04:45.810
those stories so spectacular

00:04:45.810 --> 00:04:48.540
to start with something very
basic and real and brutal,

00:04:48.540 --> 00:04:51.000
and then just go completely
into another world.

00:04:51.000 --> 00:04:53.700
I think horror allows that
specifically Stephen King horror

00:04:53.700 --> 00:04:55.140
and that's something that we did certainly

00:04:55.140 --> 00:04:56.610
with "In the Tall Grass."

00:04:56.610 --> 00:04:59.998
You gotta just dive
right in, touch the rock.

00:04:59.998 --> 00:05:02.998
(suspenseful music)

